WARNING: run_bench.py's HumanEval step executes model-generated code (he_exec.py: bubblewrap sandbox,
per-job systemd user-scope cgroup limits, per-process rlimits, timeout). This is best-effort isolation,
not a security boundary; run the audit only in a disposable VM or container. See bench/README.md.
The per-job cgroup is required by default: without `systemd-run --user --scope` he_exec.py refuses to
execute anything (SandboxUnavailable), and a launcher failure aborts the run (SandboxInfraError) rather
than counting as a failed sample. HE_EXEC_ALLOW_NO_CGROUP=1 is an UNSAFE opt-out (per-process rlimits
only, no whole-job memory limit). Self-test: python3 bench/quality/he_exec.py --self-test
Completeness: run_all.sh keeps going after a failed non-prod arm (a failed prod arm stops it) but exits non-zero
with a summary of the failed arms. Each run_all.sh invocation writes a fresh run id to runs/CURRENT_RUN; run_arm.sh
moves any existing runs/<arm> to runs/stale/<run id>/ and writes runs/<arm>/DONE only after the whole arm succeeded.
analyze.py and write_report.py use only arms whose DONE carries the current run id (skipped arms are listed on
stderr; a named incomplete arm is an error; --run-id any accepts completed arms from any run).
Request errors: any failed request (retries exhausted, non-200, malformed response, missing choices/usage) or
exception while handling an item is recorded as finish="error" and makes run_bench.py exit 1 after writing its
results (summary.json "incomplete": true, "errors" > 0); run_arm.sh then aborts without DONE, and analyze.py
refuses any arm whose summary or .jsonl results record errors, even if a DONE marker exists.

Q2 adds three CPU-prepared audit components: analyze.py corrects the BASE-degradation sign (other minus BASE > 0 with p < .05), preserves the existing Q1 tables, and appends paired BASE-minus-other non-inferiority results using paired Tango score one-sided 95% bounds (z=1.644854; they replace the earlier discordant-count Wald bounds, which collapse with few or zero discordances) and --margin-pp (default 0.5 percentage points; PASS if lower > -margin, FAIL if upper < -margin, otherwise INCONCLUSIVE); the exact two-sided McNemar calculation now uses the Python standard library instead of SciPy. run_arm.sh exports BS=1 by default, logs it in the arm.log header and forwards --max-running-requests after the wa profile; run_bench.py caps both benchmark and sanity concurrency at min(CONC, BS), recording bs/conc in summary.json (set BS in the environment, e.g. BS=2 bench/quality/run_all.sh for a future authorized server audit). prune_policy_sim.py replays immutable NPZ routes with NumPy only, sweeping singleton thresholds, independent top-1 protection and 10%/20% row-mass caps, plus joint count-two removal; caps greedily remove the lightest eligible singletons without renormalizing surviving routes, and an extra top-1-plus-20%-cap case reproduces the review comparison. From the repository root, run `python3 bench/quality/analyze.py`, `python3 -B -m unittest discover -s bench/quality -p test_q2.py -v`, and `python3 bench/quality/prune_policy_sim.py --selftest`; generate the replay table with `python3 bench/quality/prune_policy_sim.py runs/census-*.npz --output bench/quality/runs/prune_policy_sim.md`. In this checkout standard python3 lacks NumPy, so the replay was verified with the existing `$HOME/tools/sglang-rtxpro6000/.venv/bin/python -B` (Python 3.12.13, NumPy 2.3.5), without modifying the environment or starting a server. Saved results are runs/q2_quality_analysis.md and runs/prune_policy_sim.md: all 11 censuses, 22 policies each, with 49 width-matched MoE calls per step inferred from call/T cadence (51 total at W4, 63 at W16); W4 code-edit tau=.08 reproduces Astra at 14.218% mean / 38.355% p95 mass. The table's `µs/step vs 0 / .08` and `step % vs 0 / .08` columns use delta-D x 69.9 microseconds and percentages of 8,919 microseconds (W4) or 16,083 microseconds (W16). The map's 69.9 microseconds already includes 45.5 profiled calls per step, so no further calls-per-step factor is applied. These are route-only projections, not measured quality, acceptance or serving speedups; the existing Q1 runs remain BS<=2 evidence, and the new BS=1 server behavior has only been checked statically and through mocked client concurrency tests.
